I'm Rick ray I'm the director of the new  film lynching Charlie Lynch that's going  to be released on 420 this is the story  of Charlie Lynch who ran a medical  marijuana dispensary in San Luis Obispo  for quite a few years and was recently  arrested by the federal government put  on trial and not to give away too much  but very much a victim of the drug war  today I became interested in the film  because reasontv hired me to do a short  piece about Charlie Lynch I went up and  you know the producer said we're going  to be meeting someone who runs a medical  marijuana dispensary so in my mind I  kind of had thought okay he's a bit of a  STONER probably he's kind of a hippie  maybe sort of a free spirit and what I  met when I met Charlie Lynch was a  completely different kind of man the  kind of person who you you know you  would trust babysitting your kids who  looks just like an ordinary businessman  and essentially acts very much like a  regular pharmacist you know simply  wanting to dispense medicine to patients  and do it you know the right way into  illegally and that's who I met so the  disconnect was huge I went into that  interview and I thought wait a minute  this is Charlie Lynch he's never had a  traffic ticket he's never violated any  law you know and here he is about to go  to federal prison I simply can't believe  this that led me to pursue his story now  for the last four years and really get  close to his life and watch as his life  has come unglued and now he faces a  lengthy prison term what happened was  that most people in the community  accepted the dispensary they went there  for their medical medicinal marijuana  even the local police in the city where  we were hands off about it and Charlie  ran it as a tight ship people would come  in with bad IDs or what looked like fake  prescriptions and he would turn them  away he had strict rules he had rules  about people congregating outside people  consuming the substance anywhere in the  area he wanted to be a good neighbor a  good businessman  and he wanted to be responsible but the  local sheriff up in San Luis Obispo took  an instant dislike to this enterprise  and for whatever reason he sent we  believe many undercover people in to try  to get a probable cause on Charlie on a  state level and from what we gather no  one was able to get probable cause no  one was able to catch Charlie doing  something that would have crossed the  line so frustrated the sheriff called  the feds he called them and he said you  know we need we need to work together on  this and so the federal the feds came in  and rated Charlie's dispensary they  raided his home they put him into a  Federal Detention Center and now they  put him on trial and he's going to  federal prison Charlie Lynch's plight is  a small example of the great chasm  between the federal government and state  law and it's it's as if mom says you  can't put your hand in that cookie jar  and dad says hey it's perfectly okay to  put your hand in that cookie jar who are  you supposed to believe what are you  supposed to do and Charlie Lynch is a  victim he's in every man in this sense  that he has fallen into that chasm put  his hand in the cookie jar and got it  cut off by the federal government I  think a lot of us had hoped that the  Obama administration would come in and  that the prosecution and persecution of  dispensaries in California would be  relaxed in fact Eric Holder the Attorney  General made a statement saying that as  long as dispensaries were in compliance  with California law they would not be  harassed by the federal government but  the reality has been very different it's  as if they were paying lip service to  their constituency but the reality has  been the dispensaries continue to be  rated and just last week one of the  colleges that teaches about this  industry oaksterdam university was  raided I you know it astound astonishes  me that the Obama administration would  think that this is somehow appealing to  their voting bloc to be conducting these  kinds of raids so the current state of  medical  marijuana is that it is still in that  dangerous limbo between state and  federal prosecution or permission one  thing we did in this film was we got  very rare access to the the federal  marijuana distribution center at the  university of mississippi in Oxford  Mississippi and this is the one place  that's licensed by the DEA to distribute  marijuana for research we didn't think  we would ever get permission to film  there but we made an appeal to them and  surprisingly mahmood al soo Lee who runs  it gave us permission to come in and  gave us permission full access to see  how that facility is run it's it's it's  a spooky place kind of its this out  building with barbed wire around it and  you go in through security and they have  a giant bank like vault which he has a  combination and they open it and they  open this door and you walk in and you  know here's this marijuana some say it's  is pretty shitty marijuana because it's  grown by the government but nevertheless  this is the facility where they are  allowed to distribute it and conduct  research we also go up to the University  of Massachusetts and we meet Lyle Kraker  who is a botanist who's been teaching  there and has basically been trying to  get a license from the DEA to grow  research marijuana to try to get some  FDA approval of of it as a smokable  substance and a drug and his research  efforts have been largely denied and you  know that's because the University of  Mississippi has this monopoly on medical  marijuana has produced by the federal  government so we go into that and it was  fascinating to get access to that and be  able to cover it the film will be  released on 420 this is not a big  premiere in theaters we're going viral  we're going we hope people will link to  it they'll tweet about it it will be  available for download on amazon  blockbuster it'll be on hulu it'll be on  all your pay TV stations directv dish  network and so on  and it will also be for sale as a DVD on  amazon and elsewhere so we just you know  this film will be out for a long time  and we want to build awareness about  Charlie and his story so that people can  know what's happening and they can take  action and they can make a difference  this has to stop this is you know what's  happening here is putting innocent  people in the chasm between state and  federal government who are just simply  trying to do the right thing it's not  right
 

